Get Your Session ID and Server URLLog in to your Salesforce org normally.
Open the Developer Console from the gear icon.
Select Debug > Open Execute Anonymous Window.Run an Apex snippet to print your session details
:apex
System.debug('URL: ' + URL.getOrgDomainUrl().toExternalForm());
System.debug('Session ID: ' + UserInfo.getSessionId());
Check the Debug Log to copy the printed values.

Connect via API Requests
Set your request endpoint to your REST or SOAP API base URL (e.g., https://salesforce.com).
Add an HTTP header of Authorization: Bearer <Your_Session_ID>.
Add a header of Content-Type: application/json.


Connect via Frontdoor (UI Login)
Construct the frontdoor URL using your domain and session ID:
https://salesforce.com<Your_Session_ID>
Paste this URL into a browser to open an authenticated session instantly.

To connect VS Code and Salesforce CLI (SFDX) using an existing session ID, 
you use the standard authentication command while supplying the access token and your org instance URL.

1. Gather Your Credentials 
Ensure you have the exact values you copied from your Developer Console:
Session ID (the access token starting with 00D...)
Instance URL (your specific My Domain URL, e.g., https://salesforce.com)

2. Run the Auth CommandOpen the terminal in VS Code and run the sf org login access-token command.
sf org login access-token --instance-url <YOUR_INSTANCE_URL> --alias <YOUR_ORG_ALIAS>

3. Provide the Session ID
Press Enter after running the command.
The terminal will prompt you: Enter your access token:.
Paste your Session ID and press Enter.
The CLI will mask the characters for security.

4. Verify the Connection
Confirm the login was successful by listing your authorized orgs:
sf org list
Look for your chosen <YOUR_ORG_ALIAS> in the list to ensure it shows as connected.
